@page "/guides/javascript-token-client"

<DocPageMeta Title="JavaScript token client"
             Section="Guides"
             Order="40"
             Status="DocPageStatus.Draft"
             Description="Keep a plain browser page signed in to ShiftIdentity: load the token client that ShiftIdentity.Blazor ships, ask it for a token before every request, and save or clear the session."
             Keywords="token client,javascript,shiftidentity,access token,refresh token,acquiretoken,sign in,sign out,localstorage,es5,static web assets" />

<DocSection Title="What it does">
    <p>
        <code>ShiftIdentity.TokenClient</code> is a small JavaScript library for pages that are not Blazor
        components, for example a plain HTML page or a screen on an old TV browser. It keeps the session in the
        browser and hands out an access token. When the token is close to expiry, it renews the session through
        <code>POST /api/identity/v2/refresh</code> first. It runs one renewal at a time, has no background timer,
        and never navigates. It is plain ES5 with no dependencies.
    </p>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Note">
        The two files ship as static web assets of the <code>ShiftSoftware.ShiftIdentity.Blazor</code> package,
        from the first framework release after 2026.10.10.1.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Load the scripts">
    <p>
        An app that references <code>ShiftSoftware.ShiftIdentity.Blazor</code> serves both files. Load the core
        first, then the ShiftIdentity v2 adapter. Both attach to <code>window.ShiftIdentity</code>.
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Language="html" Code="@(@"<script src=""_content/ShiftSoftware.ShiftIdentity.Blazor/clients/shiftidentity-tokenclient.js""></script>
<script src=""_content/ShiftSoftware.ShiftIdentity.Blazor/clients/shiftidentity-tokenclient-issuer-v2.js""></script>")" />
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Use it in three steps">
    <p>
        Keep all token client code in one small object, as below. The rest of the page then asks it for a token
        and never touches the session itself.
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Language="javascript" Code="@(@"var AppSession = (function () {
    var store, issuer, client;

    // 1. Create one client: a store where the session is kept, and the Identity service that renews it.
    function start(identityOrigin) {
        var TokenClient = ShiftIdentity.TokenClient;
        store = TokenClient.localStorageStore('my-app.session');
        issuer = TokenClient.issuers.shiftIdentityV2({ baseUrl: identityOrigin });
        client = new TokenClient({ store: store, issuer: issuer });
    }

    // 2. Ask for a token before every API request. Exactly one handler is called.
    function getToken(handlers) {
        client.acquireToken(function (error, outcome) {
            if (error) {
                // Only a bug or broken browser storage gets here. Treat it like an outage.
                handlers.unavailable(30, error);
                return;
            }
            switch (outcome.kind) {
                case 'tokenAvailable': handlers.token(outcome.token); break;
                case 'signInRequired': handlers.signInRequired(outcome.reason); break;
                case 'temporarilyUnavailable': handlers.unavailable(outcome.retryAfter); break;
            }
        });
    }

    // 3a. After a sign-in, save the session. tokenDto is the session of a ShiftIdentity v2 answer.
    function save(tokenDto, callback) {
        var session;
        try {
            session = issuer.sessionFromToken(tokenDto);
        } catch (notASession) {
            callback(notASession);
            return;
        }
        // Clear first. A renewal of the old session that is still running is then dropped.
        client.clear(function (error) {
            if (error) { callback(error); return; }
            store.write(session, callback);
        });
    }

    // 3b. Sign out on this device.
    function clear(callback) {
        client.clear(callback);
    }

    return { start: start, getToken: getToken, save: save, clear: clear };
})();")" />

    <p><code>acquireToken</code> always answers with one of three outcomes:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <b><code>tokenAvailable</code></b>: send <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> with <code>outcome.token</code>.
            The client renews the session first when less than 60 seconds are left. While the Identity service
            cannot be reached, it still hands out the current token until that token expires.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b><code>signInRequired</code></b>: there is no session, the refresh token has expired,
            <code>clear()</code> was called, or the Identity service refused the session (for example a
            deactivated user). After a refusal the store is already empty. The page decides how to sign in again.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b><code>temporarilyUnavailable</code></b>: the Identity service did not answer and the access token
            has expired. The session is kept. The client does not call the service again for
            <code>outcome.retryAfter</code> seconds.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Every method takes a callback <code>function (error, outcome)</code>, and also returns a Promise where
        <code>Promise</code> exists. The error is only for programmer errors, such as a store that fails.
        Because there is no timer, a page that polls its API stays signed in by calling <code>getToken</code>
        before each poll.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="When the page is on another origin">
    <p>
        <code>baseUrl</code> is the Identity service's address. Use <code>''</code> when the page is served from
        the same origin. When it is not, the Identity API needs CORS for a JSON <code>POST</code> from the page's
        origin. To let the client read <code>Retry-After</code> on such answers, the API must also send
        <code>Access-Control-Expose-Headers: Retry-After</code>.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Reference">
    <p>
        The options, the store, adapter and transport contracts, and how each v2 answer maps to an outcome are in
        the client's
        <a href="https://github.com/ShiftSoftware/ShiftIdentity/blob/master/clients/javascript/README.md" target="_blank">README</a>
        in the ShiftIdentity repository.
    </p>
</DocSection>
